    slides: [
      { id: 1, title: "Azure AI Foundry — Build AI Applications", bullets: ["Microsoft's Unified Platform for Production AI Development", "Azure AI & Machine Learning — Episode 41"], script: "Welcome back. Today we explore Azure AI Foundry — Microsoft's unified platform for discovering, customizing, evaluating, and deploying AI models. Building AI applications used to require stitching together multiple services. AI Foundry brings the model catalog, prompt engineering tools, evaluation framework, and deployment infrastructure into a single developer experience at ai.azure.com." },
      { id: 2, title: "What is Azure AI Foundry?", bullets: ["Single platform: discover → build → evaluate → deploy AI apps", "Model Catalog — 1,600+ models from OpenAI, Meta, Mistral, Microsoft", "Prompt Flow — visual AI orchestration and testing", "Evaluation — measure accuracy, groundedness, safety", "Connections — link to Azure AI Search, Storage, databases", "Hub and Project structure for team collaboration"], script: "Azure AI Foundry is the answer to the question: where do I go to build an AI application on Azure? It unifies everything: the model catalog where you choose from GPT-4o, Phi-3, Llama 3, Mistral, and hundreds more; Prompt Flow for building and testing AI chains and RAG applications; evaluation tools for measuring quality metrics; and deployment infrastructure for serving your AI apps as APIs. It replaced and unified the previously separate Azure OpenAI Service portal, Cognitive Services, and Azure ML studio AI capabilities into one platform." },
      { id: 3, title: "Hub and Project Structure", bullets: ["Hub — shared infrastructure: compute, connections, security", "Project — workspace for a specific AI application or team", "Multiple projects share one hub's resources", "Hub holds: Azure OpenAI connections, AI Search, storage", "Project holds: flows, datasets, evaluations, deployments", "Hub = platform team responsibility, Project = app team"], script: "The Hub and Project hierarchy mirrors the Landing Zone model we discussed. The Hub is shared infrastructure — compute clusters, connections to Azure OpenAI, Azure AI Search, storage accounts, and the Key Vault for secrets. Multiple application teams each have their own Project connected to the Hub, sharing the infrastructure without managing it themselves. Think of Hub as the Azure subscription with shared services, and Project as the application team's workspace. This separation lets the platform team enforce security and cost governance while application teams move fast." },
      { id: 4, title: "Model Catalog", bullets: ["1,600+ models — OpenAI, Meta, Mistral, Microsoft, Cohere", "GPT-4o — best for complex reasoning, vision", "Phi-3 / Phi-3.5 — Microsoft's small, efficient models", "Llama 3 — Meta's open-source models", "Mistral — strong European open models", "Models as a Service (MaaS) — serverless pay-per-token deployment"], script: "The Model Catalog is the starting point for choosing your AI model. GPT-4o from OpenAI is Microsoft's flagship — best for complex reasoning, code generation, and multimodal tasks combining text and images. Microsoft's Phi-3 models are surprisingly capable small models — Phi-3-mini runs on-device with a fraction of the cost. Llama 3 and Mistral are open-source models you can fine-tune and deploy. Models as a Service lets you deploy any catalog model as a serverless endpoint with pay-per-token pricing, no infrastructure to manage." },
      { id: 5, title: "Prompt Flow", bullets: ["Visual graph of AI orchestration steps", "Nodes: LLM calls, Python code, tools", "Tools: web search, Azure AI Search, content safety", "Variants — test multiple prompts or model configs side by side", "Batch runs — evaluate against hundreds of test cases", "Export flow as deployable API endpoint"], script: "Prompt Flow is the AI application builder inside AI Foundry. It's a visual canvas where you compose AI applications from nodes — LLM calls, Python code snippets, and built-in tools. The AI Search tool connects your flow to a vector index for RAG. You can create variants to A/B test different prompts or model configurations in parallel. Batch runs let you evaluate your flow against hundreds of test cases and see aggregate quality metrics. When your flow is ready, you deploy it as a managed API endpoint — no containerization required." },
      { id: 6, title: "Evaluation — Measuring AI Quality", bullets: ["Groundedness — does the answer come from the provided context?", "Relevance — is the answer relevant to the question?", "Fluency — is the response well-written?", "Similarity — does the answer match a reference answer?", "Safety evaluation — detect harmful content", "Red teaming — systematically probe for failures"], script: "Evaluation is what separates AI experiments from production AI applications. AI Foundry's evaluation framework measures four key metrics for RAG and chat applications: Groundedness checks that the model's answer is supported by the retrieved context rather than hallucinated; Relevance checks that the answer addresses the question; Fluency measures language quality; and Similarity compares to reference answers. Run evaluations as batch jobs over your test set and track metric trends across iterations. Safety evaluation uses Azure AI Content Safety to detect harmful outputs before deployment." },
      { id: 7, title: "Deploying AI Applications", bullets: ["Deploy Prompt Flow as managed online endpoint", "Auto-scaling: scale out on traffic, scale to zero when idle", "A/B testing: traffic split between flow versions", "Monitoring: latency, throughput, token usage, quality metrics", "Content filtering: safe by default on all deployments", "Key-based or Entra ID auth for endpoint access"], script: "When your AI application passes evaluation, deploy it from AI Foundry directly to a managed online endpoint. The endpoint handles auto-scaling — scale out to handle traffic spikes, scale down to zero when idle to minimize cost. You can split traffic between two deployed versions of your flow for A/B testing in production. Monitor endpoint health and quality metrics in the AI Foundry portal or route to Azure Monitor. Content filtering is applied by default to all deployments, blocking harmful inputs and outputs." },
      { id: 8, title: "Summary — Azure AI Foundry", bullets: ["✅ AI Foundry — unified platform: discover, build, evaluate, deploy", "✅ Model Catalog — 1,600+ models, GPT-4o, Phi-3, Llama 3", "✅ Prompt Flow — visual AI app builder with evaluation", "✅ Evaluation — groundedness, relevance, safety metrics", "✅ Managed endpoints — auto-scale, monitoring, content filtering", "Next: Microsoft Copilot Studio"], script: "Azure AI Foundry is the production AI application platform that closes the gap between experimenting with AI in a notebook and running it reliably in production. Next episode we look at Microsoft Copilot Studio — building custom AI copilots and chatbots without needing to code the entire application from scratch." },
    ],
